  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Amendments to the Employees&#x2019; Minimum Standards of Housing, Accommodations &amp; Amenities Act 1990 (Housing Act) entered into force on 1 June 2020, while the ensuing Employees&#x2019; Minimum Standards of Housing, Accommodations and Amenities (Accommodation and Centralized Accommodation) Regulations 2020 came into force on 1 September 2020. The amendments expanded the Housing Act to cover all sectors (beyond only plantations).

Social security coverage under the Employees&#x2019; Social Security Act 1969 and Employment Insurance System Act 2017 was expanded to include domestic workers. This came into effect on 1 June 2021. The Social Security Act 1969 extended coverage of the Employment Injury Scheme to domestic workers who are Malaysian citizens, permanent residents, temporary citizens and foreign citizens and the coverage of the Invalidty Scheme was extended to domestic workers who are Malaysian citizens, permanent residents and temporary citizens. The Employment Insurance System Act 2017 extended coverage through the Employment Insurance System to domestic workers who are Malaysian citizens, permanent residents and temporary citizens in the case of loss of employment. The ILO, with assistance from the Government of the United States, supported the Ministry of Human Resources (MOHR) and social partners in amending the Housing Act through review and provision of technical comments and recommendations following international labour standards and good practices.

With support from the European Union, the ILO  provided technical support to the Ke-Arah Coalition and the International Domestic Workers Federation (IDWF) (as well as two IDWF-supported associations for Indonesian and Filipino migrant domestic workers)  to conduct consultative meetings and advocacy activities which pushed for the inclusion of domestic workers, including migrant domestic workers, into national social security schemes. Through the ILO support, civil society groups and migrant workers in Malaysia were provided with opportunities for dialogues with the Malaysian government to expand protections for migrant workers in the country. The ILO enabled migrant domestic workers voices to be heard in policymaking.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">7.5.1. Number of member States with labour migration frameworks or institutional mechanisms to protect the labour rights of migrant workers and promote coherence with employment, skills, social protection, and other relevant policies</narrative>
